    \u0438 \u043e\u0442\u0446\u043e\u043c \u0441\u043e\u0432\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0439
    \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u043a\u0438."
  interestingFactsEn: "François Viet's life is full of fascinating episodes that make his biography look like an adventurer novel, from a brilliant career as a royal adviser and an incredible cryptographic triumph to a victory in a mathematical duel and the creation of a modern mathematical language — his history is full of bright and sometimes smoking moments.\n\nThe victory over \"unreadable\" cipher and the accusations of witchcraft. Perhaps the most exciting fact of Vieta's life is his work as a crypto analyst. During the war with Spain, he was able to decipher the correspondence of Spanish King Philip II with his representatives in the Netherlands. The most complex code: Spanish cipher was incredibly complex, containing up to 600 different signs, which were constantly changed and supplemented.When the Spaniards learned that their secret code had been solved, they refused to believe in human abilities and sentenced Viet... in absentia to burn at the stake, accusing him of joining the devil and using black magic! Workoholism: Contemporarys noted his phenomenal performance. For the sake of ciphers or complex mathematical task, he could have worked for three days without sleep.\n\n♪ Mathematical duel: Like Vieth Uther Nose, the Dutchman of the second most famous episode is his triumph in the solution of the equation, which was to become a world mathematical challenge. A bold challenge: In 1594, the Dutch mathematician Adrian van Rowman proposed to solve the equation of the 45th degree by spreading it to all leading mathematicians in Europe, but missing France because he thought there were no worthy scientists.King's reply: Henry IV, after learning about this, told the Dutch Ambassador that there are maths in France and invited Vieta. The brilliant solution is that at King Wyet's reception, he not only found one root, but realized that the equation was a trigonometric task about the division of the angle, and found all positive roots.\n\nThe birth of Apollonia Gallski and the riddle of the nickname Respect for Ancient Geometry Viet showed respect for another legendary task. The task of Apollonia: Vieth solved the famous task of Apollonia Pergski in creating a circle of three circles. The name Gordon was so proud of the achievement that he was given the honorary name \"Apollonius Gallski\" (i.e. French Apollony) by his contemplators.\n\nThe thin line of faith: a Catholic among the Huguenots in the midst of violent religious wars in France, Viet held a unique position: he was born and lived most of his life among the Huguenots (French Protestants), but he himself remained a Catholic; apparently, he had little to worry about his religious differences and was able to maintain his neutrality.Witness to the massacre: On the night of the Warfolomeyev, in 1572, when the Catholics staged a mass massacre of the Huguenots, his mathematician friend Ramus and the husband of his former student died, and Viet passed a miracle through that night, which shows his remarkable luck or ability to remain out of conflict.\n\n▪ Funny and little-known details: \"Not serious\" scientist: Vieth himself did not think he was a professional mathematician. He said he was doing science in his own time for his own pleasure and published his work at his own expense. A decimal for fractions: Vieth was the first to propose the use of a decimal for the recording of decimals. This was extremely cumbersome before him (e.g. 0.3469 recorded as 3(1)4(2)(3)9(4)).Theorem, which was known before him: paradoxically, Viet's famous theorem was known in private to the ancient Babylonians and later to the Italian Cardano, but Viet's credit was that he himself brought it out in its general form and made it the basis of his new algebra, for which he received world recognition.\n\nThe mystery of the death of Vieth is covered with a vague secret. The court record says that he died in Paris in February 1603 at the age of 60, but there are unconfirmed suspicions that he was killed. If it is true, it may have been the revenge of the Spaniards for decrypting their code or the revenge of religious fanatics for his independent position.These stories make François Viet not just a dry portrait of the textbook, but a living hero of his time, a lawyer, a spy, a scientific duel and a father of modern mathematics."
